<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lester Bird</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lester Bird was an Antiguan politician, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vere-bird">Vere Bird</a>, who succeeded his father as leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigua-labour-party">Antigua Labour Party</a> and served as prime minister of Antigua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> from 1994 to 2004. His family's long dominance of Antiguan politics was marked by corruption scandals. His party lost the 2004 election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-spencer">Baldwin Spencer</a>'s United Progressive Party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lester-bird.jpg" alt="Lester Bird" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Antigua and Barbuda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lester_Bird,_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
